WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.800
Last time, a matrix could stretch,
turn and shear the whole plane.

2
00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:09.867
Now try the simplest move in a game:
slide this slime five steps to the right.

3
00:00:09.867 --> 00:00:13.167
The matrix pushes,
and the slime doesn't budge.

4
00:00:13.167 --> 00:00:16.467
Any matrix times the zero
vector gives the zero vector,

5
00:00:16.467 --> 00:00:19.167
so the origin always stays put.

6
00:00:19.167 --> 00:00:24.433
But sliding every point by a vector,
t, would send the origin to t.

7
00:00:24.433 --> 00:00:29.500
So no 3x3 matrix can move a 3D object,
wherever you put its numbers.

8
00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:31.933
The fix is one extra number.

9
00:00:32.733 --> 00:00:36.533
Give every point a fourth number,
w, and set it to one.

10
00:00:36.533 --> 00:00:40.967
So x, y, z becomes x, y, z, one.

11
00:00:40.967 --> 00:00:43.000
That's homogeneous coordinates.

12
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.367
The matrix grows to 4x4.

13
00:00:45.367 --> 00:00:49.067
Rotation and scale live
in the top-left 3x3 block.

14
00:00:49.067 --> 00:00:51.933
The last column holds the move, t.

15
00:00:51.933 --> 00:00:57.200
The bottom row is zero, zero, zero, one.

16
00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:02.767
Multiply as in the last lesson: mix the
columns, weighted by the point's numbers.

17
00:01:02.767 --> 00:01:08.367
The last column is weighted by w,
so with w equal to one, t gets added in.

18
00:01:08.367 --> 00:01:12.200
Say t is the vector (5, −2, 0).

19
00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:14.600
Take the point (1, 1, 1).

20
00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:18.100
It lands at (6, −1, 1).

21
00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:24.033
Now moving, turning and scaling are all
the same thing: matrix multiplication.

22
00:01:24.933 --> 00:01:27.200
Why does one extra number work?

23
00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:28.800
We can't draw four dimensions,

24
00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:32.167
so let's watch the same
trick one dimension down.

25
00:01:32.167 --> 00:01:37.967
A flat 2D world gets a third number, w,
and floats on the plane at height one.

26
00:01:37.967 --> 00:01:42.733
A 3x3 matrix with t in its
last column shears space:

27
00:01:42.733 --> 00:01:45.100
the plane at height one slides by t,

28
00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:47.900
while the floor at height
zero doesn't move at all.

29
00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:50.733
So inside the plane,
the whole world moved.

30
00:01:50.733 --> 00:01:53.600
A plain matrix made a translation.

31
00:01:54.833 --> 00:01:57.133
That still floor is the second gift of w.

32
00:01:57.133 --> 00:02:01.033
w equal to one marks a point,
and it gets moved.

33
00:02:01.033 --> 00:02:05.867
w equal to zero marks a direction:
the move is multiplied by zero,

34
00:02:05.867 --> 00:02:10.833
so it vanishes. Try the same
matrix on the vector (1, 1,

35
00:02:10.833 --> 00:02:13.200
1, 0).

36
00:02:13.267 --> 00:02:15.433
It comes out unchanged.

37
00:02:15.433 --> 00:02:17.100
The slime slides across the map,

38
00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:21.800
but its velocity and the way
it faces stay exactly the same.

39
00:02:21.833 --> 00:02:26.167
Forget the zero on a velocity,
and it gets moved like a point.

40
00:02:26.167 --> 00:02:30.767
The farther the slime goes, the more
wrong it points, and nothing crashes.

41
00:02:31.633 --> 00:02:35.100
Normals are directions too,
with one more catch.

42
00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:36.567
Stretch a ball twice as wide,

43
00:02:36.567 --> 00:02:40.000
and move its normals with the same
matrix: they lean with the stretch,

44
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:43.000
and stop being perpendicular
to the surface.

45
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:45.300
So the lighting comes out wrong.

46
00:02:45.333 --> 00:02:50.267
For normals, use the inverse
transpose of the 3x3 block instead.

47
00:02:50.267 --> 00:02:55.867
With only rotation, or the same scale
on every axis, you'd never notice.

48
00:02:56.967 --> 00:02:58.867
To build an object's model matrix,

49
00:02:58.867 --> 00:03:04.867
put its rotation and scale in that
corner, and multiply: T * R * S.

50
00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:10.667
Read it right to left: scale in place,
rotate around its own center,

51
00:03:10.667 --> 00:03:13.133
then move it out into the world.

52
00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:18.000
Swap it to R * T,
and the move happens first.

53
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:21.233
Then the turn swings the
slime around the world origin:

54
00:03:21.233 --> 00:03:23.800
it orbits instead of spinning.

55
00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:26.767
One last job hides in w.

56
00:03:26.767 --> 00:03:31.267
Translate, rotate and scale
never change it: points keep one,

57
00:03:31.267 --> 00:03:33.667
and directions keep zero.

58
00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:37.467
The projection matrix,
next lesson, writes a new w:

59
00:03:37.467 --> 00:03:39.967
the point's depth in front of the camera.

60
00:03:39.967 --> 00:03:44.300
Then the GPU divides x, y and z by w.

61
00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:50.100
A point with x equal to two and
w equal to five lands at 0.4.

62
00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:56.100
Twice as far, w is ten, and it lands
at 0.2: half as far from the center.

63
00:03:56.867 --> 00:04:00.200
Far things look small,
and it's just a division.

64
00:04:01.033 --> 00:04:07.033
So: a matrix always keeps the origin,
so moving needs a fourth number, w.

65
00:04:07.067 --> 00:04:10.467
A 4x4 matrix keeps rotation
and scale in its corner,

66
00:04:10.467 --> 00:04:12.933
and the move in its last column.

67
00:04:12.933 --> 00:04:15.600
w equal to one is a point, and it moves.

68
00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:19.133
w equal to zero is a direction,
and it doesn't.

69
00:04:19.133 --> 00:04:21.600
Normals take the inverse transpose.

70
00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:27.533
Build models as T * R * S,
and dividing by w makes far things small.

71
00:04:27.533 --> 00:04:31.033
Next time: model, view and projection.

72
00:04:31.033 --> 00:04:34.567
Now open the demo in this lesson,
drag the move all the way,

73
00:04:34.567 --> 00:04:37.367
and watch the blue arrows stay still.

74
00:04:37.967 --> 00:04:39.534
If this clicked, give it a like,

75
00:04:39.534 --> 00:04:42.300
and subscribe so you don't
miss the next lesson.

76
00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:43.934
See you there!
